  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1234 AM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nActivity, including the lingering risk for locally heavy rain for \nthe southern Edwards Plateau, persists into Friday morning, with \nconditions then drying out into and through the afternoon in the \nwake of the exiting shortwave. However, a secondary but weaker \nshortwave then advances from west to east in the flow aloft from \nFriday night into Halloween Saturday. This may produce additional \nisolated to scattered showers and a few storms from the Southern \nEdwards Plateau through the Hill Country. The good news is that \nmodels indicate that the rain chances look to diminish by the \nevening for the trick-or-treaters. \n\nThe remainder of the weekend and into the day on Monday primarily \nremains rain free. Sunday and Monday should also trend with the \nwarmest afternoons of the week with more ample sunshine. Daytime \nhighs range from the low 80s in the Hill Country to the mid and \nupper 80s elsewhere. Overnights remain mild and humid.\n\nWhile there are key differences within the medium range guidance, a \nmajority of the members do indicate that a more progressive upper \nlevel storm system may take shape and advance across the Desert \nSouthwest into the Southern Plains early next week. This could \nprovide the opportunity to drag a cold front across the area with \nanother chance for rain and storms. Local convective parameters \ncould support some organized convection; we'll monitor as we get \ncloser in time. Otherwise, a north-northwesterly flow behind the \nfront should help to usher in a drier and cooler airmass into the \nregion.",
